# Known replay gaps -- scripts/test-migrations.sh
# ============================================================================
# Migrations listed here are ALLOWED to fail during a from-scratch replay.
# A migration NOT listed here that fails to replay fails the build.
#
# History
# ============================================================================
# This file started with 14 entries. Some of this project's schema had never
# been created by any committed migration: db/migrations/meta/_journal.json
# references two drizzle migrations, 0002_steady_darwin and
# 0003_thin_hairball, whose .sql files are absent from the repo entirely (only
# their snapshots survive), and further objects were made directly through the
# Supabase dashboard and never captured as migrations at all.
#
# db/migrations/20251106_backfill_lost_drizzle_migrations.sql reconstructed
# what could be reconstructed: nine tables and fifteen columns, taken from
# db/migrations/meta/0004_snapshot.json, which 17ebfd47 verified equals
# production exactly. That cleared 8 of the 14 entries, and the replay now
# produces every table and column that db/schema/*.ts declares.
#
# db/migrations/20251113_backfill_campaign_characters_and_metrics.sql and
# db/migrations/20251210_backfill_starter_character_templates.sql backfilled
# the remaining three tables (campaign_characters, character_creation_metrics,
# starter_character_templates -- none modeled in db/schema/*.ts, so neither
# drizzle nor 0004_snapshot.json could supply their shape) via
# `pg_dump --schema-only -t <table>` run directly against production on
# 2026-07-25, the only trustworthy source for objects created through the
# dashboard. That closed 4 of the 6 entries outright
# (db/migrations/20260713_revoke_remaining_anon_access.sql,
# db/migrations/20251214_add_missing_fk_indexes.sql,
# supabase/migrations/20260117_update_eternal_feast_characters.sql,
# db/migrations/20260710_create_inventory_tables.sql) and surfaced what the
# other two actually need, previously unreachable because the tables couldn't
# replay at all:
#
#   * supabase/migrations/20251126_fix_remaining_table_permissions.sql also
#     grants on character_voice_mappings and character_voice_profiles --
#     different dashboard-created tables, not modeled in db/schema/*.ts,
#     out of scope for this pass. Same fix shape: pg_dump --schema-only
#     those two and backfill them the same way.
#   * supabase/migrations/20260103_seed_starter_character_templates.sql seeds
#     rows with starter_campaign_id values that must already exist, but no
#     migration ever inserts into starter_campaigns (production's 3 rows are
#     dashboard-created data, not schema). This needs a data backfill
#     (pg_dump --data-only -t starter_campaigns), not a schema migration.
#
# Closing the three tables also surfaced a schema conflict nothing had hit
# before: supabase/migrations/20251114_add_character_flow_metrics.sql
# independently creates character_creation_metrics too, but with a
# completely different, obsolete schema (flow/timestamp/campaign_id columns)
# that has nothing to do with the character_id/creation_method shape pg_dump
# captured from production. That table was clearly redesigned through the
# dashboard at some point and the transformation was never migrated. This was
# unreachable before today: the table could not replay at all, so the
# incompatibility never had a chance to surface. Production is ground truth,
# so the backfill runs first and wins; 20251114's own
# CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS then no-ops, but its CREATE INDEX/COMMENT
# statements referencing flow/timestamp fail against the real schema.
#
# ============================================================================
# RULES
# ============================================================================
# * NEVER add a newly written migration to this file. If a migration you are
#   adding cannot replay, either the migration is wrong or it depends on an
#   object that also needs a migration -- write that one too.
# * Entries may only be REMOVED. The test reports any listed migration that
#   has started passing, so this file cannot silently rot.
#
# Format: one repo-relative path per line, '#' starts a comment.
# ============================================================================

